  "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{3}\\,\u27f9\\,y={z}^{1/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3{z}^{2/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{3{z}^{2/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{z}^{2/3}}-{x}^{2}{z}^{1/3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-3{x}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nDoing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-3{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}z & =3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=3{x}^{2}.$",
